WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.320
They may not be as necessary&nbsp;
to a franchise as some others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:09.440
but these sequels are definitely worth&nbsp;
watching. And if you haven't heard of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:15.920
you aren't alone. These are the best&nbsp;
movie sequels you've never watched.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.400
Few films have had an impact as&nbsp;
great as 1939's The Wizard of Oz.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:26.320
Adapted from L. Frank Baum's novel The&nbsp;
Wonderful Wizard of Oz, not only was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:32.720
film representative of the height of technology,&nbsp;
but it confirmed Judy Garland as a bona fide star.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:39.440
The Wizard of Oz was nominated for six Academy&nbsp;
Awards, including Best Picture, and according&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:45.600
to the Library of Congress, it's been watched&nbsp;
more than any other movie. Critically beloved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:50.400
it's a true American classic that continues&nbsp;
to have an impact on audiences today.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:55.680
Forty-five years later, Return&nbsp;
to Oz finds young Dorothy Gale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:03.040
now played by Fairuza Balk, on a new adventure&nbsp;
back to Oz, only this trip is much, much darker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.520
Gone are the friendly residents&nbsp;
of Munchkinland and the familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:13.760
faces that belonged to Dorothy's Yellow Brick&nbsp;
Road companions. Emerald City lies in ruins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:25.840
overrun by the appropriately named Wheelers,&nbsp;
creatures with wheels for hands and feet.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:25.963
"Come here… chicken."

00:01:25.963 --> 00:01:30.560
To say that Return to Oz is a strange&nbsp;
second serving would be an understatement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.560
The movie has little to do with its&nbsp;
predecessor outside of its central,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:40.880
decidedly less musical, heroine. It came in&nbsp;
seventh at the box office its opening weekend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:46.880
bringing in less than $3 million on a&nbsp;
$28 million budget. Critics were split,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:53.200
with many noting its darker tone as either&nbsp;
its most positive or most negative attribute.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:58.720
Still, it's attracted a cult following in the&nbsp;
years since its release. And if a princess with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.360
a collection of heads is more your speed&nbsp;
than a wicked witch with flying monkeys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.560
Return to Oz is certainly a worthwhile endeavor.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:14.560
Brian De Palma's 1976 horror classic Carrie&nbsp;
marked the first of many Stephen King adaptations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:20.480
The story of teenage outcast Carrie White, who&nbsp;
discovers she has telekinetic abilities to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:28.160
murderous degree, Carrie is generally regarded&nbsp;
as one of, if not the best of King's adaptations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.800
and it's considered to be one of the&nbsp;
greatest horror films ever made. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:38.720
also gone on to inspire two remakes and a&nbsp;
sequel, decades after its initial release.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:44.160
While a remake of the horror classic should&nbsp;
be expected, a Carrie sequel is something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:50.480
requires a little more finesse. After all, the&nbsp;
original film ends with the teenager's death — it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:56.240
doesn't get much more final than that. Unless, of&nbsp;
course, Carrie were to have a secret half-sister&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:01.520
with the exact same untapped telekinetic&nbsp;
potential, which is the general premise for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:09.920
1999's The Rage: Carrie 2. Starring Emily Bergl&nbsp;
as said sibling Rachel Lang, the film follows the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:16.720
same general beats as its predecessor — members of&nbsp;
the in crowd play the less popular kid for laughs;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:22.080
she loses control of her abilities;&nbsp;
everything — and everyone — goes to hell.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:27.280
The Rage couldn't impress critics or&nbsp;
audiences, and its lasting legacy is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:32.720
as a disposable relic of '90s era teen&nbsp;
horror. But it's an entertaining watch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:38.240
if for no other reason than the fact that it&nbsp;
made an attempt to expand on the original's lore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:44.000
as opposed to just rehashing it altogether.&nbsp;
And Bergl is solid as Rachel, who turns out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.200
to be a more sympathetic character on account of&nbsp;
having at least some measure of social ability.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:58.000
1996's Trainspotting was an instant hit and a true&nbsp;
encapsulation of '90s indie cinema. The portrait&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:04.160
of addiction and poverty in suburban Edinburgh,&nbsp;
based on Irvine Welsh's same-named novel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:09.680
centers on a group of heroin-addicted friends&nbsp;
led by Mark Renton as they struggle with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:16.080
sobriety and everyday existence. It spoke to&nbsp;
an entire generation of lost souls, and its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:23.920
unflinching po ayal of abuse was highlighted by a&nbsp;
dark humor and an incredible soundtrack. In 1999,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:29.120
the British Film Institute named Trainspotting&nbsp;
the tenth best British film of the century.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:36.320
Two decades later, the original cast reunited&nbsp;
with director Danny Boyle for T2 Trainspotting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:41.200
which sees Renton return to his old&nbsp;
neighborhood as his marriage falls apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:46.320
he suffers a heart attack, and the impending&nbsp;
doom of middle age looms around the corner.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:50.400
"Choose Facebook. Twitter. Snapchat, Instagram,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.680
and a thousand other ways to spew your&nbsp;
bile across people you've never met."

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:59.600
This go-around, Renton and his pals find&nbsp;
themselves caught up in a legal mess revolving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:05.600
around the operation of an illegal brothel.&nbsp;
Although T2 performed well internationally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:12.880
it had a quiet opening in America, bringing in&nbsp;
less than $200,000 its first weekend. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:18.000
the film did well with critics, who praised&nbsp;
its emotionally resonant portrayal of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:23.040
group of people struggling to move forward&nbsp;
from their past mistakes. It may not be as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:28.640
innovative as its predecessor, but it still&nbsp;
speaks to those who grew up with the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:31.360
and may not yet have completely found their way.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:37.360
In the realm of science fiction, there&nbsp;
is perhaps no film more influential than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:45.840
Stanley Kubrick's 1968 masterpiece 2001: A Space&nbsp;
Odyssey. Set largely aboard the Jupiter-bound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:53.120
spacecraft Discovery One, 2001 tells the story&nbsp;
of a mysterious monolith, the group of astronauts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:58.720
sent to investigate it, and the onboard computer&nbsp;
system that ultimately turns against them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:05.600
The film was nominated for four Academy Awards,&nbsp;
winning one for Best Special Visual Effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:10.800
and is today regarded as one of the most&nbsp;
culturally significant films ever made.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:15.040
It remains a topic of discussion&nbsp;
for film fans and scholars alike.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:22.640
Peter Hyams' 1984 followup 2010: The Year We Make&nbsp;
Contact was never going to be able to reach the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:29.840
bar that 2001 had set, but it definitely tries.&nbsp;
Nine years after the Discovery One incident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:34.160
a joint US/Soviet mission is&nbsp;
sent out to uncover the truth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:38.880
2010 further explores the presence&nbsp;
and meaning of the monolith, although,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:45.120
like 2001, the audience is left with more&nbsp;
questions than answers about its true purpose.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:52.400
2010 did fairly well at the box office, opening&nbsp;
in second place with a $7 million haul. Response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:57.040
from critics was overall favorable, but its&nbsp;
biggest problem isn't that it's not a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:03.040
sci-fi movie — it really is. It's that&nbsp;
it isn't a Stanley Kubrick sci-fi movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:09.120
Taken on its own merits, 2010 turns out to be one&nbsp;
of the more enjoyable space-set films of the '80s.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:16.000
There's a benefit to following a film that's never&nbsp;
been considered a classic or a masterpiece, one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:23.040
that hasn't set any box office or awards records.&nbsp;
When expectations are low (or nonexistent),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:29.360
you can really do anything you want. 1978's&nbsp;
Attack of the Killer Tomatoes was no critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:35.200
darling — the film, about a country besieged&nbsp;
by mutated carnivorous tomatoes and the team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:41.760
of specialists tasked with saving the day, has&nbsp;
received mostly negative reviews over the years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:47.280
In spite of this, Attack of the Killer Tomatoes&nbsp;
is a bona fide cult classic that's launched an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:52.640
entire franchise of sequels, novels,&nbsp;
video games, and kids' cartoons.

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:59.120
The approach to its first sequel, 1988's&nbsp;
Return of the Killer Tomatoes!, was one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:04.960
"throw everything at the wall and see what sticks"&nbsp;
variety. A decade after the Great Tomato War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:11.440
tomatoes have been outlawed. Everything seems to&nbsp;
be going well until Professor Mortimer Gangreen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:16.160
the never-spoken-of true villain behind the&nbsp;
first film's attack, figures out a way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:20.640
reverse-engineer the music that brought&nbsp;
about his initial defeat to his favor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:27.280
transforming tomatoes into human beings. Our&nbsp;
heroes in this installment are pizza boy Chad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:33.760
tomato-turned-human Tara, Chad's best friend&nbsp;
Matt, and a poorly mutated tomato named FT.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:40.000
Like its predecessor, Return of the&nbsp;
Killer Tomatoes! is completely ridiculous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:43.840
but where Attack of the Killer&nbsp;
Tomatoes is just a parody of B-movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:53.840
Return of the Killer Tomatoes! is a parody of&nbsp;
a parody, which somehow makes it a better film.

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:57.120
(Gangrene laughs, then coughs)

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:03.040
From fourth wall breaking to unnecessary product&nbsp;
placement and fuzzy mutated pet adventures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:07.840
it's beyond anything in its&nbsp;
genre — which is a good thing.

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:11.840
The Star Trek franchise is vast and long-running,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:19.360
one that follows multiple crews and which has&nbsp;
lasted more than half a century. Since 1979,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:25.120
13 Star Trek films have been made, although&nbsp;
only three crews have made it to the big screen…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:32.160
two, if we're counting the J.J. Abrams alternate&nbsp;
timeline one as just a variation of the original.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:38.800
The film series has had its fair share of&nbsp;
ups and downs, and while one might assume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:43.760
an eighth film would have a difficult time&nbsp;
standing up against some of its predecessors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:50.080
1996's Star Trek: First Contact&nbsp;
managed to take everyone by surprise.

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:51.760
"I bring order to chaos."

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:59.120
The followup to 1994's Generations, which&nbsp;
effectively passed the motion picture torch from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:05.840
Kirk's Enterprise to Picard's, First Contact is&nbsp;
the first of the Star Trek films to focus solely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:11.760
on the Next Generation crew. It also&nbsp;
revisits one of its greatest storylines:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:18.240
the battle with the Borg. Years after Picard's&nbsp;
assimilation into and escape from the Collective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:24.320
a new Borg threat sends the crew of the Enterprise&nbsp;
back in time to save Earth from mass assimilation.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:29.280
First Contact has been hailed as one&nbsp;
of the best films in the entire series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:33.280
with critics praising both the film's&nbsp;
performances and its direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:40.160
which comes courtesy of Number One himself,&nbsp;
Jonathan Frakes. It's a truly interesting, updated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:46.480
take on the story, and having a big-screen budget&nbsp;
to tell it definitely works to the film's benefit.

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:53.840
By 2011, the world had grown weary of Sidney&nbsp;
Prescott and her seemingly never-ending parade of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.840 --> 00:11:01.680
obsessed mask-wearing murderous psychos. Scream 3,&nbsp;
which hit theaters in 2000, was panned by critics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:07.120
who claimed the film fell back on the same&nbsp;
old cliches the franchise had once destroyed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:13.600
Still, it managed to nearly make up for its&nbsp;
entire cost its first weekend at the box office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:18.000
so the studio system trudged&nbsp;
forward, greenlighting a fourth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:22.880
installment in the franchise. Hopes&nbsp;
for greatness weren't exactly high.

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:26.640
But Scream 4 turned out to be&nbsp;
more than just a passable sequel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:34.320
Returning to its roots, the film features two&nbsp;
Ghostface killers working in tandem: Jill Roberts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:40.640
Sidney's jealous, fame-hungry cousin, and&nbsp;
Charlie Walker, Jill's secret boyfriend and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:46.880
horror movie aficionado. The rules, however,&nbsp;
have changed — in this Scream installment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:50.960
we find the killers are&nbsp;
adhering to movie remake rules.

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:52.800
"Modern audiences get savvy to the rules of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:55.840
originals. So the reversals&nbsp;
become the new standard."

00:11:56.480 --> 00:12:00.240
It's a small change from the&nbsp;
franchise's original formula,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:05.280
but it's enough to make this particular&nbsp;
sequel stand out. Having killer stars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:10.560
Emma Roberts and Rory Culkin inject some&nbsp;
much-needed fresh blood into the franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:16.080
is an added bonus — their performances are what&nbsp;
ultimately makes the movie, and it's fun to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:22.160
check out Roberts play with villainy prior to her&nbsp;
memorable turn on American Horror Story: Coven.

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:29.680
When it hit theaters in 1978, no one&nbsp;
suspected that John Carpenter's Halloween,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:34.560
a low-budget slasher about a boogeyman&nbsp;
in a spray-painted Captain Kirk mask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:39.920
and mechanic's coveralls who stalks a babysitter&nbsp;
and kills all her friends along the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:45.360
would become the phenomenon it did. The film&nbsp;
set the stage for what much of horror would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:50.880
go on to look like in the following years,&nbsp;
and it's since become not only a classic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:55.440
but required viewing for anyone even&nbsp;
remotely interested in the genre.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.440 --> 00:13:01.200
Michael Myers is an icon, and it's hard to&nbsp;
imagine the world of Halloween movies without him.

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:05.680
But it wasn't always meant to be this&nbsp;
way. Carpenter's intention was to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:11.200
have the Michael Myers story end with the&nbsp;
first film — after its success, Carpenter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:17.440
quote, "had to" write the sequel, but, as he&nbsp;
explained to The Hollywood Reporter in 2015,

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:22.320
"I thought that we were done with telling stories&nbsp;
about Michael Myers and the guy in the mask.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:27.520
I thought there's not much more to say. So&nbsp;
we thought we'd come up with a new story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:34.000
every year. We could call it Halloween, but it&nbsp;
didn't have to do anything with Michael Myers."

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:40.960
And so in 1982, we got Halloween III: Season&nbsp;
of the Witch, a film that swaps out Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:47.040
Myers for a piece of Stonehenge and some cursed&nbsp;
Halloween masks, and which tells the story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:53.040
one man's plot to sacrifice a bunch of children&nbsp;
in order to bring about a new age of witchcraft.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:58.560
Halloween III may not have been a hit with critics&nbsp;
who'd expected another Michael Myers installment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:04.240
but it's since developed its own fanbase — one&nbsp;
that doesn't need a masked maniac at Halloween.

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:10.560
There's no denying the overwhelming&nbsp;
appeal of 1984's horror comedy Gremlins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:16.080
Centered on a family who unwittingly unleashes&nbsp;
a horde of mischievous monsters upon the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:22.240
small town of Kingston Falls, Gremlins&nbsp;
combines holiday fantasy with outright terror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.240 --> 00:14:26.640
taking on a consumer culture with&nbsp;
a little water and some sunlight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:32.160
It proved to be a massive hit, raking&nbsp;
in over $12 million its opening weekend.

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:39.120
A few years later, the cast and crew reunited,&nbsp;
and, hoping to strike gold twice, Gremlins 2:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:45.600
The New Batch hit theaters in June of 1990. While&nbsp;
the basic premise of the original story is here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:52.400
for the most part, Gremlins 2 is a movie all its&nbsp;
own. Both films are horror comedies, but Gremlins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.400 --> 00:14:58.320
2 takes the story in a more lighthearted direction&nbsp;
than its predecessor, relying more on parody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:04.320
for laughs. It's over the top in its comedic&nbsp;
excess — Joe Dante, who directed both films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:10.160
told Bloody Disgusting in 2015 that he&nbsp;
never wanted to make a sequel but was given,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.160 --> 00:15:16.240
quote, "three times the money" along with the&nbsp;
freedom to do whatever he wanted. He explained:

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:22.000
"So I made Gremlins 2, which was essentially about&nbsp;
how there didn't need to be a sequel to Gremlins."

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:25.280
"I can't believe this. I mean,&nbsp;
this is worse than the first one."

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:28.480
"We just show these movies,&nbsp;
madame, we don't make them."

00:15:29.680 --> 00:15:35.760
Regardless of how it came to be, Gremlins 2 is&nbsp;
one of those sequels that fans should hold as near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:41.520
and dear to their hearts as the original, even if&nbsp;
it is just making fun of itself the entire time.

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:48.480
Demonic possession has served as Hollywood horror&nbsp;
fodder for decades. The most famous of these films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:56.080
is, of course, 1973's The Exorcist, William&nbsp;
Friedkin's take on William Peter Blatty's 1971&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.080 --> 00:16:02.720
novel that centers on Regan, a young girl who&nbsp;
falls under the control of a demon named Pazuzu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.720 --> 00:16:09.360
and on Father Damien Karras, a priest struggling&nbsp;
with his faith who's tasked with saving her. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:15.840
film wound up being a hit at the box office,&nbsp;
raking in nearly $200 million domestically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:21.920
and it became the first horror movie to be&nbsp;
nominated for Best Picture at the Academy Awards.

00:16:21.920 --> 00:16:28.720
Obviously, there was a market for Pazuzu, and&nbsp;
in 1977, he and Regan made a comeback with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:35.280
The Exorcist II: The Heretic. That film, which&nbsp;
attempted to expand upon the demonic lore of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:42.160
first with psychic healers and doppelgängers, was&nbsp;
a failure both critically and financially. But in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:48.960
1990, Blatty returned to the franchise, serving&nbsp;
as both writer and director of The Exorcist III.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.520 --> 00:16:56.480
Ignoring the events of The Heretic, The Exorcist&nbsp;
III follows William Kinderman, a police lieutenant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.480 --> 00:17:02.400
investigating a series of deaths that look like&nbsp;
those committed by a now-dead serial killer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:08.560
Jason Miller reprises his role as Karras,&nbsp;
who, after having survived the Regan exorcism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:11.600
is now being used as a killer conduit.

00:17:11.600 --> 00:17:17.440
While not without its faults, The Exorcist&nbsp;
III is a vast improvement on its predecessor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:23.120
It's thoughtful, and both Scott and Miller are&nbsp;
exceptional. In an unfortunate turn of events,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.120 --> 00:17:29.600
however, Blatty was forced to add an exorcism&nbsp;
sequence to the end, forever marring a film that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.600 --> 00:17:34.240
would have otherwise been a haunting and&nbsp;
cerebral followup worthy of his original.

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:42.000
When Wes Craven's A Nightmare on Elm Street hit&nbsp;
theaters in 1984, it was like nothing horror fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:48.240
had ever seen before — a killer targeting teens&nbsp;
in their dreams, a community of adults behind a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:53.440
massive coverup, and a villain so terrifying&nbsp;
that he kept both the film's characters and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.440 --> 00:18:00.960
its audience awake at night. Robert Englund built&nbsp;
his entire career around Freddy Krueger, a role he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:06.720
credits for making him, quote, "an international&nbsp;
actor overnight," and since first donning that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.720 --> 00:18:12.880
knifed glove, he's gone on to play the killer&nbsp;
seven more times. As the franchise went on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:19.600
however, it became a parody of itself, and Freddy&nbsp;
was relegated to little more than a caricature.

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:25.440
But Craven always was the kind of writer/director&nbsp;
who thrived on turning things on their head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:31.920
and, like he did with the horror genre in 1984, he&nbsp;
flipped the narrative of his original masterpiece&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.920 --> 00:18:38.480
a decade later with Wes Craven's New Nightmare.&nbsp;
Reuniting original cast members as real-life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:43.920
versions of themselves who wind up terrorized&nbsp;
by a demonic force posing as Freddy Krueger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:48.880
New Nightmare has almost nothing to do with&nbsp;
the original Nightmare on Elm Street narrative.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.880 --> 00:18:52.400
Instead, it examines the horror industry itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.400 --> 00:18:57.440
as well as the legacy of Craven's original&nbsp;
film and the franchise it created.

00:18:57.440 --> 00:19:00.960
"The problem comes when the story dies.&nbsp;
And that can happen in a lot of ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:05.280
it can get too familiar to people, or someone&nbsp;
waters it down to make it an easier sell.”

00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:13.760
It's meta at its very best, and it in fact serves&nbsp;
as a sort of warm-up to Craven's 1996 hit Scream.

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:19.520
The approach proved successful with both&nbsp;
critics and audiences. Wes Craven's New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.520 --> 00:19:24.560
Nightmare is considered one of the best in the&nbsp;
entire series, second only to the original.

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:31.920
Comedies in the early aughts were often over the&nbsp;
top, raunchy, and ridiculous. Stoner comedies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:37.040
which were exceptionally popular at the time,&nbsp;
were all of these things... but with an added&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:43.280
layer of unlikely drug-induced hijinks.&nbsp;
Enter Harold &amp; Kumar Go to White Castle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.280 --> 00:19:49.440
a 2004 film that follows the titular characters&nbsp;
on a pot-fueled quest to satiate their late night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:54.960
munchies. The pair are thrust into a series of&nbsp;
misadventures but ultimately find their way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.960 --> 00:20:00.320
thanks in part to Neil Patrick Harris.&nbsp;
While not an Oscar contender by any means,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:05.680
Harold &amp; Kumar Go to White Castle received&nbsp;
generally favorable reviews upon its release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.680 --> 00:20:09.360
mostly on account of its&nbsp;
likable leads and edgy humor.

00:20:09.360 --> 00:20:16.160
Although its 2008 followup, Harold &amp; Kumar&nbsp;
Escape From Guantanamo Bay, was decidedly less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:24.080
successful, John Cho and Kal Penn reunited once&nbsp;
more in 2011 for A Very Harold &amp; Kumar Christmas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:29.600
a 3D holiday film that revisits the duo two&nbsp;
years after having gone their separate ways.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:36.160
Like the films that came before it, A Very Harold&nbsp;
&amp; Kumar Christmas tends toward lowbrow humor, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.160 --> 00:20:42.720
not at all to the extent that Guantanamo Bay did.&nbsp;
Instead, Christmas leans into the holiday spirit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.720 --> 00:20:46.400
tempering its crass comedy&nbsp;
with genuine feel-good moments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:50.080
That isn't to say that it will ever&nbsp;
become a holiday classic, but it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:55.360
an entertaining final installment to a franchise&nbsp;
that understood when it was time to say goodbye.

00:20:56.480 --> 00:21:02.160
There's much to be said for Robert Zemeckis'&nbsp;
1985 time travel comedy Back to the Future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.160 --> 00:21:06.400
Telling the story of high schooler Marty&nbsp;
McFly, who, with the help of inventor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.400 --> 00:21:11.600
Doc Brown, travels back in time to save his&nbsp;
parents' marriage and his future existence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:17.600
Back to the Future is, to this day, one of the&nbsp;
most beloved and original movies ever made.

00:21:17.600 --> 00:21:21.840
It was both a critical and financial&nbsp;
success, going on to become the highest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.840 --> 00:21:28.800
grossing film of 1985. It was nominated for&nbsp;
four Academy Awards, including Best Screenplay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:35.440
and The New York Times lists it as one of the&nbsp;
Best 1000 Movies Ever Made. There are two sequels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:41.200
and while 1989's Back to the Future Part II&nbsp;
is the one that's remembered most by fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.200 --> 00:21:45.280
it's Back to the Future Part III that's&nbsp;
the forgotten gem of the franchise.

00:21:45.280 --> 00:21:47.440
"Great scott!"

00:21:47.440 --> 00:21:53.440
After learning of Doc's death in 1885 at the&nbsp;
hands of Biff Tannen's great-grandfather Mad Dog,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:59.680
Marty travels back to the Old West in order to&nbsp;
save him. Part III has a much simpler narrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:59.680 --> 00:22:06.160
than Part II, but it's helped along with a fun and&nbsp;
interesting setting, a locomotive time machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.160 --> 00:22:11.840
and a love interest for Doc that, quite frankly,&nbsp;
was well deserved. The film was also a bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.840 --> 00:22:17.120
hit with critics than the prior installment, with&nbsp;
the general consensus being that its paring down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.120 --> 00:22:22.720
of plot elements worked in its favor. It may not&nbsp;
be the cultural powerhouse that the original is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.720 --> 00:22:27.840
but it definitely keeps from falling into&nbsp;
the franchise trap of unworthy sequels.

